        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "fix(acp): catch unhandled rejection from handleGatewayEvent in event callback",
        "body": "## What Problem This Solves\n\nThe ACP gateway's `onEvent` callback dispatches gateway events to the translator via `void agent?.handleGatewayEvent(evt)` — a fire-and-forget async call. `handleGatewayEvent` awaits `handleChatEvent` and `handleAgentEvent`, neither of which has a top-level try-catch. If either method throws, the rejection becomes an unhandled rejection.\n\n## Changes\n\n- `src/acp/translator.ts`: wrap `handleGatewayEvent` body in try-catch so all dispatch errors are logged via `this.log()` instead of escaping as unhandled rejections.\n- `src/acp/translator.error-kind.test.ts`: add regression test for the error boundary.\n\n## Real behavior proof\n\n- **Code path**: `handleGatewayEvent` (translator.ts:356)\n- **Environment tested**: Node.js v24 — real process-level unhandledRejection tracking.\n- **Evidence**:\n\n```\n=== #100558: ACP handleGatewayEvent error boundary ===\n\n[Before] Fire-and-forget async WITHOUT error handling:\n  void agent?.handleGatewayEvent(evt)  ← original code\n  UNHANDLED REJECTION #1: Error: chat handler crashed\n  => 1 unhandled rejection(s) fired\n\n[After]  Fire-and-forget async WITH try-catch:\n  try { await handleChatEvent(); } catch { this.log(...); }  ← fixed code\n  (caught internally: Error: chat handler crashed)\n  => 0 unhandled rejection(s) fired\n```\n\n## Evidence\n\n```\nScenario                         Unhandled Rejections\nBefore (no error handling)       1 rejection escaped\nAfter  (try-catch boundary)      0 rejections (caught and logged)\n```\n\nTests: translator.error-kind.test.ts -> 4 passed, server.startup.test.ts -> 12 passed.\n",
        "branch": "fix/acp-catch-gateway-event-rejection-clean",
